<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haarlem</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haarlem is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, capital of the province of North Holland, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Chartered in 1245, it was a residence of the counts of Holland and a centre of brewing, linen bleaching and cloth. It held out against a seven-month Spanish siege in 1572–1573 before surrendering, and in the Golden Age it was home to painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a>. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> railway linked it to Amsterdam in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haarlem.jpg" alt="Haarlem" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fryslan0109 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HaarlemGroteMarkt1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Haarlem lies on the river Spaarne, about 20 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and near the coastal dunes. It stands on a thin strip of land above sea level known as the strandwal, which connects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> to Alkmaar, where people struggled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to the west and the waters of the IJ and the Haarlem Lake to the east. The city became wealthy from the tolls it collected from ships and travellers on this busy north–south route. For centuries it was the centre of the tulip bulb-growing district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest mention of Haarlem dates from the 10th century, and a Christian parish church has stood there since the 9th. The first city walls were built in 1270. Beer brewing was important from the 15th century, when there were 100 breweries in the city, and clean water from the dunes also served the bleaching trade.</p><p>After the Geuzen conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brielle">Brielle</a>, Haarlem began to support them, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> sent an army north under Don Fadrique. It besieged the city on 11 December 1572. The defences were commanded by the governor Wigbolt Ripperda, and a widow, Kenau Simonsdochter Hasselaer, helped defend the city with some three hundred other women. A great fire on the night of 22 to 23 October 1576 began in a brewery near the weighhouse. The Spanish left in 1577.</p><p>To restore the economy and attract workers, the council promoted the arts and showed tolerance for diversity of belief. Haarlem declined in the 18th century as trade gravitated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. On 19 January 1795 a bloodless revolution removed the city's administrators the night William V fled the country, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> was proclaimed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Haarlem is the capital of the province of North Holland. Teylers Museum, on the Spaarne, is the oldest museum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the Frans Hals Museum houses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> master paintings. The city is known for its hofjes, almshouses built around courtyards, of which 19 survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haarlem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The country’s first railway, between Amsterdam and <b>Haarlem</b>, had opened in 1839, symbolising a slow turn towards modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The palace they served was vast: Olfert Dapper’s book of 1668, compiled from Dutch reports, compares it to the town of <b>Haarlem</b>, with apartments for courtiers and galleries whose wooden pillars were covered with cast metal plaques.</blockquote><a href="/societies/benin">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</a></li><li><blockquote>It is first attested in 1573, sung during the siege of <b>Haarlem</b>, and in a German translation of the same year; the oldest complete Dutch text is in a Geuzenliedboek (Beggars' songbook) printed in 1577–1578.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>At a bulb auction in <b>Haarlem</b>, buyers failed to appear and prices for tulip contracts crashed. 'Tulip mania' is often cited as the first recorded speculative bubble, though its damage is now thought modest.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-3">3 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orangists took <b>Haarlem</b>'s resistance as a heroic example at the sieges of Alkmaar and Leiden.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts drained peat bogs, built dykes and founded towns such as <b>Haarlem</b>, Delft, Leiden and Dordrecht.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
